---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import site from "../data/site.json";
import { u } from "../lib/path.js";

const img = (p) => u(p);
const pi = site.contacts[0];

const description =
  "The Churchman Lab at Harvard Medical School is recruiting postdoctoral fellows and rotation students. What we look for, what projects are open, what to put in your email, and what to expect from the process.";
---

<Base title="Join Us" description={description}>
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom:30px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Join us</p>
      <h1>We are hiring postdocs and taking rotation students</h1>
      <p class="lede prose">
        We take people from biology, chemistry, physics, computer science, mathematics and engineering. If you want
        to answer a question about gene regulation with our novel set of methods or develop your own, our lab is the
        place to do it!
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <figure style="margin:0">
        <img
          src={img("/images/site/join-art.jpg")}
          alt="Synchronized mitochondrial and cytosolic translation programs, a painting by Leidy Churchman"
          loading="lazy"
          style="border-radius:3px"
        />
        <figcaption class="figcap">
          Leidy Churchman, <em>Synchronized mitochondrial and cytosolic translation programs</em> (2016)
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two-col">
      <div>
        <h2>Open positions</h2>

        <div class="qa">
          <h3>Postdoctoral fellows</h3>
          <p>
            We are always actively recruiting. Projects are available across both research programs — the nuclear
            mRNA life cycle and mitonuclear co-regulation — and in method development that cuts across both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="qa">
          <h3>Rotation students</h3>
          <p>
            We take rotation students from Harvard's graduate programs, including BBS, MCO, Chemical Biology,
            Systems Biology and Biological Sciences in Public Health. Email Stirling to set up a meeting to discuss
            potential rotation projects! There’s room for 1-2 students to join the lab in Summer 2027.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="box accent">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">How to apply</h3>
          <p style="font-size:.94rem"><strong>Postdocs.</strong> Email Stirling (<a href={`mailto:${pi.email}`}>{pi.email}</a>) with:</p>
          <ul style="font-size:.94rem;color:var(--ink-2);padding-left:1.1em">
            <li>your CV</li>
            <li>
              a short paragraph on why this lab, ideally naming an open question that would be well addressed in
              our lab and why it grabs your attention
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p style="font-size:.94rem">
            You do not need a full research proposal, just a good starting point for a conversation.
          </p>
          <p style="font-size:.94rem;margin-bottom:0">
            <strong>Rotation students.</strong> Just email Stirling, no formal application required, but your CV and
            a brief note on “why us” can help shape our first conversation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="box" style="margin-top:24px">
          <h4 style="margin-top:0">What happens after you email</h4>
          <p style="font-size:.94rem;color:var(--ink-2);margin-bottom:0">
            Stirling replies within two weeks, and if she doesn’t, please email again. Hiring needs change faster
            than this website is updated, so if there is space, promising applicants have a video call; candidates
            then visit for a day, give a seminar, and meet everyone in the lab.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>What we look for</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        <div>
          <h4>Curiosity, resourcefulness, and dedication with an open mind</h4>
          <p style="font-size:.94rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
            Many of our projects begin because the existing method cannot answer the question. Or a foundational
            question is stalled, and our existing tools can push it forward. Or the data don’t add up, so a new
            hypothesis emerges. We look for people who can identify the next tractable step, pursue it rigorously
            and revise their assumptions when the evidence requires it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Comfort being the only one in the room with your background</h4>
          <p style="font-size:.94rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
            We all have different backgrounds, so none of us understands everything. We work together to help fill
            each other's gaps. Some of us come in with tremendous bench experience, and some with none at all. Some
            of us have never used a programming language and for some of us, it’s all we know. A willingness to
            learn is all we ask, and we firmly believe that our sum is greater than its parts! And besides, we all
            love learning new things.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Care about other people's science</h4>
          <p style="font-size:.94rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
            Nearly every paper on our <a href={u("/publications")}>publications page</a> has multiple lab authors,
            which is reflected in how we naturally love to jump in and help with projects while leading our own. We
            look for people who help maintain a rigorous, collaborative and supportive environment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>How we use AI</h2>
        <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">
          We are AI-curious, AI-aware, and AI-cautious. We are excited about AI’s potential to advance scientific
          work, and we welcome people who are eager to use these tools—or open to learning about them—thoughtfully.
          We use AI for exploration and assistance, not as evidence or authority, and we independently verify the
          work it helps produce. Learning to use AI critically is becoming an important scientific skill, and we want
          everyone in the lab to gain thoughtful experience with it while continuing to develop their own analytical,
          coding, and writing abilities.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two-col">
      <div>
        <h2>Funding and support</h2>
        <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">
          While lab funding is available for trainees without an external fellowship, many people in the lab hold
          their own fellowships: currently the Helen Hay Whitney, an NIH F32, an NSF Graduate Research Fellowship
          and a Parkinson's Foundation fellowship are represented. We support fellowship applications actively and
          early.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Where people go afterwards</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          Lab alumni have gone on to faculty positions at the University of Washington, Boston University, the
          Université de Sherbrooke and the Max Planck Institute for Molecular Genetics, and to senior scientific
          roles in biotech and consulting.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">
          <a class="more" href={u("/team#alumni")}>See where everyone went →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="box">
        <h2 style="margin-top:0">Our values</h2>
        <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">
          We strongly affirm that anyone is welcome in the lab, regardless of race, gender identity, sexual
          orientation, religion, ethnicity, nationality, or ability. We strive to create a supportive lab atmosphere
          so that everyone feels welcome and can fulfill their potential.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
